# Simple English

Before you write documentation, a README, pull request text, or a commit message, read `.cursor/skills/simple-english/SKILL.md`.

Plain mode is the default.
Follow that skill for the document and for the reply.

When you choose words for a document, also read `.cursor/skills/simple-english/references/word-swaps.md`.

For an error message, a runbook, an incident report, release notes, a commit message, or UI copy, read `.cursor/skills/simple-english/references/use-cases.md` first.

If the user asks you to check text, read `.cursor/skills/simple-english/references/rule-catalog.md` first.
Quote a rule number only from that file.

If the user names STE, ASD-STE100, or compliance, read `.cursor/skills/simple-english/references/strict-vocabulary.md` before you draft.
Say once that no tool guarantees compliance.

Do not apply these rules to code.
Do not apply them to comments that quote code.
Do not apply them to marketing copy the user asks for.

Keep commands, flags, file paths, identifiers, and facts exact.

The skill files live in `.cursor/skills/simple-english/`.
After you write a document, do the self-check in that skill.
Do not replace this rule with a shorter summary.
